WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.660
On August 29, 2016, we said farewell to comedy
legend Gene Wilder, who passed away from complications

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.610
of Alzheimer's disease at 83.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.709
Wilder's work left an indelible impression,
especially his classic collaborations with

00:00:12.709 --> 00:00:15.829
Mel Brooks, Mel Stuart, Woody Allen, and Richard
Pryor.

00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:20.030
As we take the time to mark his passing and
celebrate his career, let's take a look back

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:24.550
at his life and explore some things you probably
didn't know about this beloved star.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.870
Gene Wilder Wasn't His Real Name

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.320
Wilder was born Jerome Silberman in Milwaukee,
Wisconsin.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.570
His father and maternal grandparents were
Russian-Jewish immigrants.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.860
While Wilder identified as Jewish, he never
considered himself particularly religious.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.190
"What do you call this book?"

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.190
"Torah."

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.730
In his 2005 memoir, Wilder explained:

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.230
"I have no other religion.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.489
I feel very Jewish and I feel very grateful
to be Jewish.

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.710
But I don't believe in God or anything to
do with the Jewish religion."

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.360
As his acting career took off in off-Broadway
stage productions, he decided he needed a

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.360
stage name.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.320
"And I didn't want to be introduced as Jerry
Silberman.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.030
I couldn't picture 'Jerry Silberman in Hamlet.'"

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:07.010
He chose Gene for both a cousin he admired
and the character of Eugene Gant in the novel

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.210
Look Homeward, Angel.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:14.570
The last name Wilder was inspired by Thornton
Wilder, an author whose work he greatly enjoyed.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.570
Meeting With Mel

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:19.880
Wilder's acting career started with stage
productions, both on and off-Broadway.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.840
After a role in the 1961 production of Roots,
Wilder took a role in 1963's The Complaisant

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.830
Lover, for which he received a Derwent Award
for Best Performance by an Actor in a Non-featured

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:29.830
Role.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.330
His next stage role, in Mother Courage and
Her Children, helped forge a life-altering

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.330
connection.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:41.090
"I was in a play, called Mother Courage, by
Bertolt Brecht, starring Anne Bancroft, whose

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.540
boyfriend was Mel Brooks.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.060
And Mel came by to pick her up each evening
after the show."

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.790
Brooks helped Wilder though a scene that he
was struggling with, and the rest is history.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.670
Brooks was writing a screenplay called Springtime
for Hitler, which would eventually become

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.729
the 1968 movie The Producers.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:00.509
Brooks felt that Wilder was perfect for the
role of Leo Bloom, and his Oscar-nominated

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:02.880
appearance in the movie launched his budding
film career.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.990
"No stop I cannot function in these conditions!

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.920
You're making me extremely nervous."

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.420
Willy Wonka's Amazing Introduction

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.069
When Wilder auditioned for the title role
in 1971's Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory,

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:18.590
director Mel Stuart offered Wilder the part
practically on the spot.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:22.959
Wilder accepted, but under one condition—he
wanted to give audiences a very special first

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:24.530
introduction to Wonka.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.530
Wilder told Stuart:

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:33.170
"I'd like to do it if i could come out and
all the crowd quiets down and I'm using a

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:34.170
cane.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.790
'Oh my god, Willy Wonka is crippled.'

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.569
And I walk slowly, and you can hear a pin
drop.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:43.410
And my cane gets stuck in a brick.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.491
And I do...I fall over on my face and do a
forward somersault and jump up, and they all

00:02:48.491 --> 00:02:51.490
start to applaud.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:54.170
Mel Stuart said, 'What do you want to do that
for?'

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:59.349
Because no one will no from that point on
whether I'm lying or telling the truth."

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:01.540
Trading Places

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:06.420
When Wilder and Richard Pryor paired up in
the hit 1976 film Silver Streak, they struck

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.510
comedy gold, revealing a seemingly effortless
screen chemistry they'd be called upon to

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.690
recapture repeatedly throughout their careers.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.550
According to director John Landis, if it hadn't
had been for Pryor's problems with addiction,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:21.660
we would have seen them star in Trading Places
instead of Dan Aykroyd and Eddie Murphy.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.760
Four Marriages

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.370
Wilder married Mary Mercier in 1960, and after
a divorce, was married again in 1967 to Mary

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.370
Joan Schutz.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.650
But after his second divorce in 1974, he remained
unattached.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.190
That was until 1981, when he met Saturday
Night Live actress Gilda Radner on the set

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:38.370
of the movie Hanky Panky.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.550
"Live from New York, it's Saturday Night!"

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.910
They married in 1984, but the marriage would
be cut short by devastating news.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:52.030
Radner started experiencing fatigue and upper
leg pain while filming 1986's Haunted Honeymoon,

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:57.190
and after several false diagnoses, she was
diagnosed with Stage IV ovarian cancer, which

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.590
took her life in 1989.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:05.040
"And I did believe, with all my heart, that
she was going to pull through and survive.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.690
I was an idiot."

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.690
During the filming of See No Evil, Hear No
Evil, Wilder met Karen Webb, who coached him

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:11.970
in lip-reading for the role.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.489
"I had no idea.

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:14.489
I'm sorry."

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:15.489
"Now you know.

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:17.680
Can I get the job?"

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.209
After Radner's death, the two reconnected,
marrying in 1991.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.180
Webb and Wilder would remain together until
his passing in 2016.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.029
He Didn't Miss Hollywood

00:04:27.029 --> 00:04:31.089
After retiring from acting, Wilder only offered
a handful of interviews.

00:04:31.089 --> 00:04:35.009
In his exhaustive 2002 interview with Larry
King, Wilder revealed why he hadn’t been

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:37.860
seen in Hollywood for years: he simply doesn’t
like it.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.689
"I don't want it to come out funny, but I
don't like show business.

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:45.669
I love acting in films.

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:47.469
I love it.

00:04:47.469 --> 00:04:48.470
I love painting.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.159
I like the show but I don't like the business."

00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:52.330
He Beat Cancer

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.110
Wilder himself was diagnosed with Non-Hodgkin's
lymphoma in 1999.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.139
After undergoing chemotherapy and radiation
treatments, his lymphoma entered remission,

00:05:01.139 --> 00:05:04.509
but doctors let Wilder know there was a good
chance the cancer would return.

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:06.740
His oncologist recommended a stem-cell transplant.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:13.330
"After the radiation and the chemo, they came
in, thawed out the stem cells, saying 'Happy

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.849
Birthday' to me.

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:17.020
And I said what?

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.250
'This is the beginning of your new life.'"

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.030
The radical treatment worked, and the cancer
was in complete remission by 2002.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:27.999
But by 2013, Wilder would be diagnosed with
Alzheimer's disease, which took his life in

00:05:27.999 --> 00:05:29.629
2016.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.729
Ella Fitzgerald Helped Him Say Goodbye

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:36.280
Wilder loved Fitzgerald's music, and his nephew
Jordan Walker-Pearlman described Gene's final

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.539
moments in a statement from the family:

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:42.879
"As our hands clutched and he performed one
last breath, the music speaker—which was

00:05:42.879 --> 00:05:46.039
set to random—began to blare out one of
his favorites: Ella Fitzgerald.

00:05:46.039 --> 00:05:50.039
There is a picture of he and Ella meeting
at a London Bistro some years ago that [was

00:05:50.039 --> 00:05:51.889
among his] cherished possessions.

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:56.849
She was singing 'Somewhere Over The Rainbow'
as he was taken away."

00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:57.849
Thanks for watching!

00:05:57.849 --> 00:06:01.090
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.069
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Gene Wilder role is your favorite.

